{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals dusting temple steps like confetti. No guidebook could prepare me for the quiet magic of Gion at dawn: wooden machiya houses, the whisper of a geisha’s obi, and the scent of matcha drifting from a hidden teahouse.\n\nI wandered Nishiki Market, where grilled octopus, pickled plums, and matcha mochi tempted every sense. A fishmonger smiled, handed me a sample of fresh uni, and said, “Eat it like the Japanese do—slowly, with gratitude.” I did.\n\nIn Tokyo, the city pulsed like a living circuit board. Shibuya’s scramble crossing overwhelmed me—until I noticed how everyone moved with eerie harmony, no shouting, no pushing. I ate ramen at a tiny stall in Shinjuku, slurping noodles so loudly the chef nodded in approval. “It means you’re enjoying it,” he said.\n\nI took the bullet train to Hakone, where Mount Fuji loomed like a snow-capped god. Onsen baths steamed under starlight; I soaked in silence, surrounded by bamboo and the distant chime of temple bells. No phones. No noise. Just steam, warmth, and the feeling of being utterly, peacefully present.\n\nIn a rural village, I stayed with a family who served kaiseki—each dish a seasonal poem. The grandmother taught me to fold a paper crane. “It’s not about perfection,” she said. “It’s about intention.”\n\nJapan didn’t just show me beauty—it taught me stillness. In a world of noise, I learned to listen: to the rustle of tatami, the clink of ceramic, the space between heartbeats.\n\nI left with a suitcase full of tea, a crumpled paper crane in my pocket, and a quiet mind.\n\nThree weeks. One lifetime.", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.3133279